3. What Problem Does Docker Solve?

Docker helps with:

Dependency conflicts
Environment differences
Faster deployments
Easier scaling
Better resource utilization
Portability across environments
4. What is a Container?

A container is a running instance of a Docker image.

Think of it like this:

Image  --->  Container

Blueprint ---> House

Recipe -----> Cooked Food

Class ------> Object
Interview Answer

A Docker container is a lightweight, isolated environment that runs an application and all of its dependencies.

5. What is a Docker Image?

An image is a read-only template used to create containers.

Example:

ubuntu image

↓

docker run ubuntu

↓

Ubuntu Container

You can create many containers from the same image.

6. Docker Architecture
             Docker Hub
                  ↑
                  |
           Pull / Push Images
                  |
        -----------------------
        |    Docker Engine    |
        -----------------------
             |           |
        Container 1   Container 2
Components
Docker Client: where you type Docker commands.
Docker Engine (Daemon): performs the requested operations.
Docker Hub: stores Docker images.
Docker Registry: a repository for images (Docker Hub is a public registry).
7. Docker vs Virtual Machine
Virtual Machine	Docker Container
Full OS	Shares host OS kernel
Heavy	Lightweight
Boots in minutes	Starts in seconds
Higher memory usage	Lower memory usage
Larger disk size	Smaller images
Hypervisor required	Docker Engine required
Example

A VM is like renting an entire apartment.

A Docker container is like renting one furnished room in a shared building. You get your own private space while sharing common infrastructure.

8. Install Docker on Ubuntu

Update packages:

sudo apt update

Install Docker:

sudo apt install docker.io -y

Start Docker:

sudo systemctl start docker

Enable Docker on boot:

sudo systemctl enable docker

Check status:

sudo systemctl status docker
9. Verify Docker Installation

Check Docker version:

docker --version

Expected output (version number may differ):

Docker version 28.x.x

Detailed information:

docker info
10. Your First Docker Command

Run the official test container:

docker run hello-world

What happens?

Docker checks whether the hello-world image exists locally.
If not, it downloads the image from Docker Hub.
Docker creates a container from the image.
The container runs, prints a success message, and exits.

This demonstrates the basic Docker workflow:

docker run hello-world

